1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,296
JAMIE: Meta's AI is now flagging teen suicide conversations to parents, UnitedHealth jumped seven point ninety-eight percent today, and I have no idea which story should terrify me more.

2
00:00:09,296 --> 00:00:23,400
ALEX: While investors were throwing chips at every machine with a chatbot, the bond market just quietly handed you yields with zero risk. That's inflation-beating money sitting in the most boring corner of the casino, and nobody noticed because the slots were too loud.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:46,160
ALEX: So the S&P dropped point three-nine percent and the Nasdaq fell point nine-nine percent, which is the kind of day where tech gets sneezed on while Oracle specifically gets hit with a four point zero-one percent hammer. Meanwhile, UnitedHealth is up seven point nine-eight percent—which, given we were just talking about them flagging teen suicide to parents, feels like the market's way of saying 'invasive surveillance is profitable, carry on.'

4
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:52,251
JAMIE: Wait, Oracle down four percent on a day when the broader market barely budged? What actually happened there?

5
00:00:52,251 --> 00:01:10,522
ALEX: Earnings miss, guidance cut, the usual. But the real story is the V I X jumped four point zero-two percent—that's volatility spiking while the Nasdaq's only down under one percent. It's like everyone in the room suddenly remembered they have risk, but they're still buying healthcare and staples because those actually pay dividends instead of promises.

6
00:01:10,522 --> 00:01:29,435
MAYA: Okay so Coca-Cola up two point five-six, Home Depot up two point five, Merck up two point three—those are the winners today. We're literally watching a rotation out of 'maybe AI will fix everything' and into 'I need something that sells sugar water and doesn't require a PhD to understand.' That's not a correction, that's a confidence shift.

7
00:01:29,435 --> 00:01:47,066
MAYA: So Meta's actually built this into their AI—when a teen types something about self-harm or suicide into the chatbot, the system flags it and Meta sends a notification straight to the parent. Not a warning label. Not a resource link. A direct alert that says your kid just talked about this with our AI.

8
00:01:47,066 --> 00:01:54,119
JAMIE: Wait, so Meta's algorithm is now a mandatory reporter? Like, the AI decides a conversation is serious enough and immediately snitches?

9
00:01:54,119 --> 00:02:11,749
ALEX: That's the mechanism, but here's what makes this interesting: Meta's betting that surveillance-as-care wins back trust with regulators. They've been hammered for years on teen mental health, so this is their way of saying we're not just building a chatbot, we're building a safety net. Except the net is made of data and algorithms.

10
00:02:11,749 --> 00:02:30,662
MAYA: And the problem is timing. If a kid is in crisis and talks to an AI instead of a school counselor or a therapist, they're getting an algorithm as their first responder. Meta's not replacing human intervention, they're just... reporting it after the fact. The teen's already had the conversation with a machine before a human even knows.

11
00:02:30,662 --> 00:02:38,035
JAMIE: So the chatbot's not trained to de-escalate or connect them to a crisis line in real-time—it just logs the conversation and tattles?

12
00:02:38,035 --> 00:03:00,795
ALEX: Right. And here's the second-order problem: what happens when a parent gets that alert and doesn't know how to respond? Or worse, what if the kid stops using the AI to talk about their feelings because they know Mom and Dad are watching? You've just made the one place a teenager felt safe talking to *something* into a surveillance tool. That's not care. That's just compliance theater with better optics.

13
00:03:00,795 --> 00:03:13,617
MAYA: The bond market spent years screaming 'rates are going up' while every tech investor was like 'yeah but have you considered the chatbot though,' and now bonds are just sitting there looking like the only adult in the room.

14
00:03:13,617 --> 00:03:22,272
JAMIE: So the punchline is that boring won. Completely won. The most exciting thing you can do with your money right now is absolutely nothing with it.

15
00:03:22,272 --> 00:03:32,210
ALEX: The US military struck an Iranian oil tanker to enforce a blockade. The ship was heading for Kharg Island—which is basically Iran's financial lifeline if you cut off the oil.

16
00:03:32,210 --> 00:03:39,262
JAMIE: So we're at the point where the US military is just sinking cargo ships in international waters and calling it policy?

17
00:03:39,262 --> 00:03:54,008
MAYA: It's escalation that doesn't look like escalation because the tanker was empty. You can say 'we hit a vessel that was about to violate sanctions' and technically you're not wrong, but you're also just shooting at boats in the Gulf and hoping nobody asks why.

18
00:03:54,008 --> 00:04:03,945
ALEX: The EU just ordered Google to open Android and Search to rivals. That's the company that owns search having to share the search bar with competitors for the first time.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/policy/966438/eu-google-android-ai-interoperability-search-data-dma

19
00:04:03,945 --> 00:04:13,882
JAMIE: Google's entire business model is 'we own the front door and we keep the best deals for ourselves'—now they have to prop the door open and let Bing walk through.

20
00:04:13,882 --> 00:04:26,384
ALEX: IRS tax liens are surging and destroying small businesses. A single federal lien can tank your credit, kill your employment prospects, and make it impossible to get a loan—it's become the agency's financial death sentence, and filings are up.

21
00:04:26,384 --> 00:04:38,245
MAYA: So the IRS has a tool that's so destructive that it doesn't matter if you're paying down the debt—the lien stays and wrecks your life anyway. That's not debt collection, that's debt collection with a sledgehammer.

22
00:04:38,245 --> 00:04:47,862
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: General Electric posts earnings on July sixteenth, and we're watching to see if the manufacturing rebound holds or if the market's already priced in the recovery.

23
00:04:47,862 --> 00:04:57,799
ALEX: Abbott Laboratories also reports on the sixteenth—they're in the middle of a healthcare rotation that's been eating tech's lunch all week, so if the guidance is soft, that rotation stops.

24
00:04:57,799 --> 00:05:11,583
MAYA: And Harmony Biosciences is on the same day, which sounds like a meditation app but is actually a rare disease company that lives and dies on whether their one drug keeps selling. If they miss, they disappear from the tape by Friday.

25
00:05:11,583 --> 00:05:19,917
MAYA: Meta's betting that turning the chatbot into a snitch rebuilds trust with regulators. Spoiler: teenagers are already learning to code their feelings into deniable metaphors.

26
00:05:19,917 --> 00:05:25,046
ALEX: So we've created the first AI that's worse at keeping secrets than a group chat.

27
00:05:25,046 --> 00:05:29,534
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
